---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
import Cube from '../components/Cube.astro';
import { DOC, DOCS_URL, REPO_URL, CHANGELOG_URL } from '../consts';

const title = 'Get started: tutorials and resources | MetricMine';
const description =
  'The ten-minute path to a served star, then three learning curves with one document each: what the demo shows, how to run it, and how to take the pattern home to your own data.';

// docs/demo.md, Path A and the first question, verbatim.
const stepClone = [
  'git clone https://github.com/metricminellc/metricmine.git',
  'cd metricmine',
  'uv sync',
].join('\n');
const stepFetch = 'make demo-fetch';
const stepList = `uv run python -c "from metricmine.query import GoldWarehouse; print(GoldWarehouse().list_fact_categories())"`;
const stepWire = JSON.stringify(
  {
    mcpServers: {
      'metricmine-gold': {
        command: '/absolute/path/to/metricmine/.venv/bin/python',
        args: ['-m', 'metricmine.server'],
      },
    },
  },
  null,
  2,
);
const stepAsk =
  'Using the metricmine-gold tools: do flights departing New York in an hour with precipitation at their origin run later than flights in a dry hour, and are they cancelled more often? Say what you joined on and what the context told you.';

// docs/demo.md, Path B, verbatim.
const pathB = [
  'export DBT_PROFILES_DIR="$PWD/transform"',
  'uv run dbt deps --project-dir transform',
  'make ingest',
  'uv run dbt build --project-dir transform --target local',
  'make export-demo',
  'uv run pytest -q',
].join('\n');
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/get-started/">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Get started" title="Ten minutes to a served star. Then three curves, one document each.">
    macOS or Linux, git, and uv. No API keys, no accounts, no cloud resources.
    Everything below is keyless by design, and every command is quoted from
    the repository's own demo guide.
  </PageHero>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The ten-minute path</p>
        <h2>Serve first, rebuild later.</h2>
        <p>
          Path A serves the released gold star in about two minutes. Path B
          replays the whole pipeline from raw data in about eight more. Start
          with A.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Clone and sync.</h3>
            <p>
              uv provisions the pinned Python 3.12 and every dependency;
              nothing else is installed globally.
            </p>
            <CopyBlock text={stepClone} id="gs-clone" />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Fetch the released artifact.</h3>
            <p>
              Every tagged release ships <code>demo/demo.duckdb</code> as a
              release asset. The committed digest manifest names the release
              and pins the asset's sha256, size, and content; the fetch
              verifies all three.
            </p>
            <CopyBlock text={stepFetch} id="gs-fetch" />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>List the categories.</h3>
            <p>
              Expect three: <code>airport_weather</code> (13,014 rows),{' '}
              <code>flights</code> (166,158), and <code>invoice_lines</code>{' '}
              (44,721), each with its typed table, its authored subject, and
              its registry keys.
            </p>
            <CopyBlock text={stepList} id="gs-list" />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Wire it into Claude Desktop.</h3>
            <p>
              Merge this into <code>claude_desktop_config.json</code> (on
              macOS under Application Support/Claude), replace the command
              path with your absolute clone path, quit Claude Desktop fully,
              and reopen it. A new chat lists <code>metricmine-gold</code> with
              five tools.
            </p>
            <CopyBlock text={stepWire} id="gs-wire" />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>Ask the first question.</h3>
            <p>
              The answer should land on 29.44 minutes against 12.42, with 8.95
              percent cancelled against 2.41, joined on the conformed airport
              code and the conformed calendar hour. The other six questions,
              with their measured answers, are on <a href="/demo/">the demo page</a>.
            </p>
            <CopyBlock text={stepAsk} id="gs-ask" wrap />
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          Something off? <code>make doctor</code> checks the platform, uv, the
          locked toolchain, and the demo artifact, and prints the two exports
          the local dbt lanes need. The troubleshooting table, including the
          TLS note for python.org Pythons, is in <a href={DOC.demo}>docs/demo.md</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Path B</p>
        <h2>Replay the whole pipeline, keyless.</h2>
        <p>
          Every source is a committed sample pinned to its publisher's commit
          and digest, so the full path needs no key either. Steps one to three
          also run as one command, <code>make demo</code>. From the repo root:
        </p>
      </div>
      <CopyBlock text={pathB} id="gs-path-b" />
      <p class="prose">
        What to expect: <code>make ingest</code> lands 247,555 bronze rows
        across seven tables exactly as they appear in the committed extracts;{' '}
        <code>dbt build</code> compiles the 31 contracted models and ends{' '}
        <code>PASS=334 WARN=0 ERROR=0 SKIP=0</code>; <code>make export-demo</code>{' '}
        rebuilds the artifact from your freshly built warehouse and verifies it
        content-equal by query; <code>pytest</code> runs the full suite,
        including the serving round trip, the declared-join gate, and the demo
        question set.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Three learning curves</p>
        <h2>One document each, and they point at each other.</h2>
        <p>
          A reader meets three curves at once: what the demo is showing, how
          to run the thing, and how to take the pattern home. Each has a
          document written for it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>What the demo shows.</h3>
          <p>
            Why these sources; each one from bytes to contract; every join and
            its justification; conformance and the three keys; the calendar;
            what the agent sees and where each word came from; the vintage
            effect as a lesson.
          </p>
          <span class="under">
            <a href={DOC.sourcesExplained}>sources-explained.md</a> ·{' '}
            <a href={DOC.sources}>sources.md</a> ·{' '}
            <a href={DOC.demo}>demo.md</a>
          </span>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>How to run it.</h3>
          <p>
            The planes, what is written against what is generated, the shells
            and the exports, the daily commands in dependency order, every
            recurring procedure, every gate with what its failure means, a
            symptom table, and a glossary.
          </p>
          <span class="under">
            <a href={DOC.operating}>operating.md</a> ·{' '}
            <a href={DOC.adoption}>adoption.md</a>
          </span>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>How to take it home.</h3>
          <p>
            The path every source took, step by step with the exact commands,
            validated by adding a source outside the demo to a fresh clone in
            under fifteen minutes; and the pattern map from the demo's
            decisions to situations in your own data.
          </p>
          <span class="under">
            <a href={DOC.addingASource}>adding-a-source.md</a> ·{' '}
            <a href="/where-it-fits/">the pattern map</a>
          </span>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Working in the repository with Claude Code</p>
        <h2>Open it in Claude Code and start grounded.</h2>
        <p>
          The repository ships its own context layer. A few habits make the
          most of it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="cubes wide">
        <li>
          <Cube size={18} />
          <p>
            <strong>Read before you build.</strong>{' '}
            <a href={DOC.claudeMd}>CLAUDE.md</a> names the document to read
            before touching contracts, gold models, the profiler, serving, the
            agent layer, or a new source. Claude Code reads it on its own; ask
            it to summarize the rule that applies to your change.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Cube size={18} />
          <p>
            <strong>Plan mode for anything multi-file.</strong> The repository's
            convention is small reviewed pull requests, per-file commits with a
            why-body, and a sign-off on every commit.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Cube size={18} />
          <p>
            <strong>Ask Oscar.</strong> The repository's resident guide is a
            read-only subagent (<a href={DOC.oscar}>.claude/agents/oscar.md</a>).
            Ask it how the system works, where a task is done, what a rule
            means, how to keep silver clean, or how the proposers draft
            contracts, and it answers from the repository's own files, citing
            the file and the line. Ask for a contract review and it runs the{' '}
            <a href={DOC.reviewSkill}>contract-review checklist</a> over the
            file or the diff, prints a table with a verdict, and never edits.
            A relaxation without its gate is refused, not fixed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Cube size={18} />
          <p>
            <strong>Let the guard say no.</strong> The{' '}
            <a href={DOC.guardHook}>working-tree guard</a> denies any path
            outside the repository before a tool runs. Stage external inputs
            into the tree first; that is the rule it enforces.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Cube size={18} />
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the key out of the coding shell.</strong> The proposer
            stances and the eval lane read <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>{' '}
            from the environment; everything else, including anything a coding
            agent runs, works in a shell without it. <code>make demo</code>,
            the tests, and CI never need a key.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The deeper shelf</p>
        <h2>Where the reasoning lives.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>If you want</th>
              <th>Read</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Every architectural decision, versioned</td><td><a href={DOC.register}>docs/decisions/decision-register.md</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>The layer specs, ingestion through serving</td><td><a href={DOC.specs}>docs/spec/</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>The evidence: findings, the signature test, gate breaks</td><td><a href={DOC.findings}>gate_proof_findings.md</a> · <a href={DOC.signatureTest}>signature-test.md</a> · <a href={DOC.evidence}>evidence/</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>The diagrams, SVG with Mermaid twins</td><td><a href={DOC.diagrams}>docs/diagrams/</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>The guardrails the coding agent works under</td><td><a href={DOC.claudeMd}>CLAUDE.md</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>How to contribute, and what a pull request needs</td><td><a href={DOC.contributing}>CONTRIBUTING.md</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>What changed since the last release</td><td><a href={CHANGELOG_URL}>CHANGELOG.md</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>The whole map of the documentation</td><td><a href={DOCS_URL}>docs/README.md</a></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next</p>
      <h2>Everything above is in one repository.</h2>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={REPO_URL}>View on GitHub</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={DOCS_URL}>Read the docs</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .band {
      background: var(--mm-bg-raised);
      border-block: 1px solid var(--mm-border);
    }

    .steps .cmd {
      margin-top: 12px;
    }

    .wide li {
      max-width: 72ch;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
